issue='The source states this as a general matrix-construction question over fields, without choosing a benchmark field or a complete encoding of explicit entries. Fix the field/characteristic (and any allowed field extensions), the representation and bit cost of output entries, and whether factors are over that field or its algebraic closure. Rational entries with binary numerators/denominators and finite-field entries are possible distinct conventions, not interchangeable assumptions. The sparsity target, arbitrary intermediate width and polynomial-time uniform construction requirement are otherwise recoverable.'
save(i,dict(title='A polynomial-power lower bound for explicit depth-two linear circuits',formal='Construct an explicit family of n×n matrices A_n whose depth-two linear complexity is Ω(n^{1+ε}) for some constant ε>0. For the intended field F, define L₂,F(A)=min{nnz(B)+nnz(C): m≥1, B∈F^{n×m}, C∈F^{m×n}, A=BC}. The desired quantifiers are: one deterministic matrix-construction algorithm, constants ε,c>0 and n₀, and a fixed polynomial running-time bound, such that for every n≥n₀ the algorithm outputs A_n and every factorization A_n=BC of arbitrary intermediate width m has nnz(B)+nnz(C)≥c n^{1+ε}. The source requires construction in time polynomial in n, rather than mere existence or subexponential construction. The field, entry encoding and possible extension field for the factors still need to be fixed.',definitions='nnz(M) is the number of entries of a matrix M that are not the zero element of its field. A factorization A=BC represents the exact linear map x↦Ax: the first layer computes Cx and the second computes B(Cx). Each nonzero coefficient gives a weighted wire, so the total number of wires is nnz(B)+nnz(C). The number of intermediate gates m is not required to be n or even at most n. Coefficients of B and C are arbitrary elements of the specified factor field; they may cancel. No bound on coefficient magnitudes or coefficient description lengths is part of this circuit-size measure. Neither factor is required to be square, invertible, nonnegative, a transpose of the other, or sparse in every individual row or column. Equality A=BC is exact, without numerical approximation or Boolean-semiring interpretation. For explicitness, n may be supplied in unary (equivalently, time is measured as a polynomial in n, not in log n), and one uniform deterministic algorithm must output all n² entries in the chosen representation. This is a bound on constructing the hard matrices, not a uniformity restriction on the competing factorizations, which may be chosen separately for every n. A finished version must charge the representation length and arithmetic needed for the selected entry model.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Once the field and representation conventions are fixed, provide one uniformly polynomial-time constructible family and an unconditional proof of the stated sparsity lower bound for all its depth-two factorizations, or refute that existence assertion in the same model. A lower bound for only nonnegative, bounded-coefficient, symmetric or invertible factorizations does not suffice. Neither a nonconstructive hard matrix nor a construction taking exp(n^a) time for fixed a>0 meets polynomial-time explicitness.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The unknown family is part of the requested answer',text='The source asks for an explicit hard matrix family, not for a lower bound on a specified candidate. The missing part of the extracted sentence identifies depth-two linear complexity. In matrix language, this means total sparsity of two unrestricted rectangular factors.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Why the construction requirement matters',text='Theorem 2 constructs hard matrices for constant-depth linear circuits in subexponential time, and records correspondingly large entry descriptions or extension degrees. This improves what can be constructed with that larger resource budget, but does not give a polynomial-time explicit family for the target. The paper’s quadratic lower bounds concern additional restrictions on the factors.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A link to broader lower-bound questions',text='The source explains connections to matrix rigidity and to linear data-structure representations. These links make general sparse factorization a meaningful model of computational complexity even at depth two. Removing restrictions on coefficients and factors is essential to that model.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Later results retain the unconditional gap',text='Alman and Li discuss barely superlinear unconditional lower bounds for explicit matrices and derive conditional polynomial-power bounds for particular Kronecker-power transforms. Their conditional statements do not settle the requested unconditional construction. Their results also concern stronger consequences of lower bounds for selected candidates.',citation='kronecker2025'),dict(title='September 2026 progress is an upper bound',text='Ye’s revised preprint reports smaller depth-two circuits for the disjointness matrix. This advances constructions of efficient circuits for that particular transform; it neither constructs a family with the required unconditional lower bound nor rules out such a family.',citation='disjointness2026')],why='Would establish a substantial explicit lower bound for an elementary algebraic computation model, beyond restrictions that forbid cancellation or special factor shapes, with connections to rigidity and linear data structures.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Subexponential-time hard-matrix constructions and quadratic lower bounds for restricted factorization models are established; polynomial-time explicit general depth-two lower bounds remain the concluding target.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025',text='Conditional lower bounds and consequences for selected matrix families are obtained, while the general unconditional explicit lower-bound gap is still described.',citation='kronecker2025'),dict(date='2026-09-03',text='A revised preprint improves disjointness circuit upper bounds; this is distinct from the target construction of a provably hard family.',citation='disjointness2026')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the source’s explicitness definition, circuit-size model, construction theorem and full concluding question. Checked the 2025 lower-bound discussion and the September 2026 disjointness preprint’s main result. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no unconditional polynomial-time explicit construction settling the unrestricted question. Field and encoding choices remain to be specified for this card.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',notes=['Recovered the n×n explicit-family target, polynomial-power wire bound and unrestricted rectangular factors.'],remaining_issue=issue),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A basic unrestricted linear-circuit lower-bound question linked to matrix rigidity and data-structure complexity. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note=issue,working_summary=dict(sentences=['The goal is to construct an n×n matrix family for which every two-factor representation has many nonzero entries in total.','The desired bound is c n^{1+ε} for a fixed positive ε, with arbitrary intermediate factor width and unrestricted coefficients.','One uniform algorithm must output the matrices in polynomial time in n.','Known subexponential constructions and lower bounds for restricted factorizations do not meet all these requirements.','The field and encoding conventions still need to be fixed, and the checked 2025–2026 results do not supply the requested unconditional family.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','kronecker2025','disjointness2026'])),notes=['Recovered polynomial-power depth-two linear-circuit lower bound, rather than generic matrix factorization hardness.','Defined total nonzero count, arbitrary width, exact field equality and nonuniform competing factors.','Specified uniform polynomial construction time while leaving field/entry conventions visible.','Removed source technique advice and checked recent conditional lower bounds and upper bounds.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
